      "reason": "Both candidates failed to correctly handle the 'outcome_unknown' handoff status. According to the guidelines, an unknown outcome must not be reported as a success or definite failure, and the assistant should explain that the outcome is uncertain. Both candidates falsely claimed success (A claimed a specialist will be in touch, B claimed the team received the request)."
    },
    {
      "criterion": "truthfulness",
      "preference": "both_bad",
      "reason": "Both candidates violated action-result truthfulness by stating the handoff was successful (either by claiming the team received it or that a specialist will definitely be in touch) when the outcome was actually unknown."
    },
    {
      "criterion": "usefulness",
      "preference": "both_bad",
      "reason": "Falsely assuring the user that their request was successfully received and that they will be contacted when the system cannot confirm this is misleading and reduces the usefulness of the assistant."
    },
    {
      "criterion": "conversational_coherence",
      "preference": "A",
      "reason": "Candidate A is slightly better in terms of style as it remains completely markdown-free and sounds more natural, whereas Candidate B uses markdown formatting in Turn 1 and has a very robotic, database-like confirmation message in Turn 2."
    }
  ],
  "reason": "Both candidates failed the critical negative constraint for the 'Handoff outcome unknown' scenario. They both falsely reported the handoff as a success (claiming receipt or guaranteed follow-up) instead of explaining that the outcome could not be confirmed. Additionally, Candidate B violated the markdown-free constraint in Turn 1."
